Given the 6 fully detailed papers (AlphaProof, Aristotle, DeepSeek-Prover-V2, Goedel-Prover-V2, Hilbert, Kimina-Prover) plus references from their cross-citations, let me produce the field guide.

```json
{
  "innovation_patterns": [
    {
      "pattern": "Informal-Formal Iterative Feedback Loop",
      "description": "Rather than treating autoformalization as a one-shot translation, the highest-performing systems create a dialogue between informal reasoning (high-level strategy, decomposition) and formal verification (ground-truth error signals). Informal reasoning proposes proof structure; formal verification identifies exactly where it fails; the informal reasoner revises. This loop iterates until convergence or a depth/iteration budget is exhausted. The power comes from exploiting the complementary failure modes: informal reasoners hallucinate details but think strategically, while formal provers are syntactically precise but strategically myopic.",
      "examples": ["Aristotle", "DeepSeek-Prover-V2", "Hilbert", "Goedel-Prover-V2"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Verifier-as-Swiss-Army-Knife",
      "description": "The Lean compiler/verifier is not just a binary pass/fail oracle — the top systems exploit it simultaneously as (a) a reward signal for RL training, (b) a curriculum generator (extracting unsolved subgoals from failed proofs via extract_goal, or generating valid-but-unproven statements), (c) a self-correction oracle (feeding compiler error messages back as structured context), and (d) a data quality filter (validating autoformalized statements). Systems that treat the verifier as only one of these underperform those that exploit all four roles.",
      "examples": ["AlphaProof", "Goedel-Prover-V2", "DeepSeek-Prover-V2", "Kimina-Prover"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Subgoal Decomposition as Data Synthesis Strategy",
      "description": "Decomposing hard problems into subgoals (via 'have' statements with 'sorry' placeholders in Lean 4) serves double duty: it is both a proof search strategy AND a synthetic training data pipeline. When an informal reasoner decomposes a problem and a prover solves each subgoal, the composed result becomes a training example that teaches unified informal+formal reasoning. This insight — that decomposition is simultaneously a search method and a data generation method — is a genuinely reusable pattern.",
      "examples": ["DeepSeek-Prover-V2", "Hilbert", "Aristotle"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Test-Time Compute Scaling (TTRL/TTT/Self-Correction)",
      "description": "Allocating additional compute at inference time — whether through test-time reinforcement learning on bespoke curricula of simplified variants (AlphaProof), retraining on the model's own search traces (Aristotle), or iterative self-correction with compiler feedback (Goedel-Prover-V2) — reliably improves performance on the hardest problems. This works because formal verification provides an exact reward signal that makes test-time optimization well-defined, unlike domains where test-time scaling hits evaluation noise.",
      "examples": ["AlphaProof", "Aristotle", "Goedel-Prover-V2"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Autoformalization-as-Curriculum (Noisy is Fine)",
      "description": "Autoformalization doesn't need to be semantically faithful to the source problem to be useful for training. Any syntactically valid formal statement is a legitimate training problem for an RL agent with a verifier reward. This reframes autoformalization from a translation accuracy problem to a curriculum diversity problem, decoupling the data bottleneck from translation fidelity.",
