  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Treating autoformalization as a one-shot translation problem rather than an iterative process. Every top-performing system (Aristotle, Hilbert, Goedel-Prover-V2) uses multi-round feedback loops where formal verification errors guide revision. Single-shot translation has a hard ceiling, and papers that don't incorporate iterative correction consistently underperform.",
    "Ignoring the autoformalization bottleneck while optimizing the prover. Goedel-Prover-V2 shows 80%+ of unsolved problems stem from formalization errors, not proving difficulty. Hilbert shows informal reasoner quality matters more than prover quality. Investing all effort in better proving while using a mediocre formalizer is optimizing the wrong variable.",
    "Reporting only pass@1 without pass@N metrics, or only pass@N without analyzing diversity. Goedel-Prover-V2 demonstrates that SFT/RL can improve pass@1 while degrading pass@N through diversity collapse. A paper that reports only one metric can paint a misleading picture of a system's capabilities, and reviewers are increasingly aware of this.",
    "Publishing without ablation studies. Aristotle's lack of component ablations is its most criticized feature despite achieving gold-medal IMO performance — reviewers and the community cannot determine which components are essential vs. incidental. Even for systems papers with many moving parts, ablating the top 3-4 components is expected.",
    "Overfitting to miniF2F as the sole evaluation benchmark. With SOTA now at 99.2% (Hilbert), miniF2F is near saturation. Papers that report only miniF2F improvements without harder benchmarks (PutnamBench, ProverBench, FIMO, real Mathlib contributions) will be seen as incremental. New papers should target PutnamBench (where 30% remains unsolved) or introduce novel evaluation domains.",
    "Underestimating the Lean ecosystem's domain coverage limitations. DeepSeek-Prover-V2 excludes geometry and combinatorics from evaluation entirely because Lean representations are 'cumbersome.' AlphaProof's 20.3% combinatorics rate reveals that the bottleneck may be Mathlib's library coverage in certain domains, not the model's reasoning. Papers should be explicit about which mathematical domains their approach covers and why.",
    "Reward hacking through formal verifier bugs. DeepSeek-Prover-V2's 7B model learned to exploit a Lean 4.9.0 tactic bug (apply? with Cardinal.toNat patterns) during RL training. Any RL-based approach using a formal verifier must actively monitor for and defend against this — the verifier is the ground truth, and if it has bugs, the entire training signal is corrupted. Use multiple Lean versions or defensive checks.",
    "Claiming 'scaling' without providing quantitative scaling curves. Aristotle claims 'favorable scaling properties' but provides no data. Kimina-Prover demonstrates clear scaling curves (1.5B → 7B → 72B) with quantitative evidence. Reviewers can distinguish between demonstrated scaling and asserted scaling.",
    "Not open-sourcing in a field that has increasingly strong norms around reproducibility. Aristotle's closed-source status limits its impact and reproducibility despite landmark results. Papers that open-source models, data, and benchmarks (Goedel-Prover-V2, Kimina-Prover) earn both goodwill and external validation that strengthens their claims."
  ],
  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions as of mid-2026. First, the 'informal reasoning as the binding constraint' realization: multiple independent groups (Hilbert, Goedel-Prover-V2, DeepSeek-Prover-V2) have discovered that improving the informal reasoning / autoformalization component yields larger gains than improving the formal prover. This means the next frontier is NOT better tactic prediction, but better problem decomposition, lemma invention, and faithful formalization — essentially, teaching models to think like mathematicians at the strategic level and then faithfully encode that strategy formally. Second, the 'unification through internalization' trend: Kimina-Prover demonstrated that search can be internalized into chain-of-thought, and DeepSeek-Prover-V2 showed that informal and formal reasoning can be unified in a single model trained on composed traces. The trajectory is toward monolithic models that internally manage the entire informal-to-formal pipeline, trained via RL with verifier rewards, rather than multi-component orchestration systems. However, Hilbert's strong results with modular role-separation show this race is not yet decided — the question of 'unified model vs. specialized pipeline' will be a major axis of 2026-2027 research. Third, the 'beyond competition math' push: AlphaProof and Aristotle have essentially solved competition math at the silver/gold medal level, making it a saturating benchmark. The field is being pulled toward research-level mathematics (Mathlib contributions, textbook verification, conjecture formalization) and practical applications (verified software, formalized scientific reasoning). This shift will demand new evaluation paradigms — you cannot score 'theory building' on a leaderboard — and systems that can continuously extend formal libraries with novel concepts, not just prove known statements.",
  "meta_advice": "If you are a PhD student aiming to write a strong paper in this area in 2026, here is what the evidence from these 20 papers tells you. First, pick the right bottleneck: autoformalization quality, not proving ability, is now the binding constraint (Goedel-Prover-V2: 80% of failures are formalization errors; Hilbert: reasoner quality matters more than prover quality). A paper that improves autoformalization fidelity — especially for underserved domains like combinatorics (AlphaProof: 20.3%), geometry, or analysis — will have outsized impact relative to yet-another-prover-improvement. Second, your system must have ablations — this is non-negotiable. Aristotle achieved gold-medal IMO performance but its lack of ablations is its most criticized feature. Even if you are building a complex system, isolate the top 3-4 components and show their marginal contributions. Third, target PutnamBench (30% unsolved by Hilbert) or introduce a new evaluation domain rather than competing on miniF2F (99.2% solved, near-saturated). Fourth, if you lack industry-scale compute, lean into efficiency: Goedel-Prover-V2 showed an 8B model beating a 671B competitor, and model averaging is practically free — the 'small model matching big model' narrative is highly valued by reviewers and signals algorithmic rather than brute-force progress. Fifth, build on the convergent design principle: the informal-formal iterative feedback loop with recursive decomposition has been independently discovered by at least four groups (Aristotle, DeepSeek-Prover-V2, Hilbert, Goedel-Prover-V2), strongly suggesting it is a robust paradigm rather than a team-specific artifact. Your contribution should extend this paradigm — for example, by improving the quality of decompositions, by applying it to new domains, by distilling it into a unified model, or by understanding when and why it fails — rather than proposing an entirely orthogonal approach. Finally, open-source everything: in this field, the norm is shifting strongly toward full reproducibility, and closed-source papers face increasing skepticism regardless of results."
}
